# Bindings and schema pinning

This page describes generating typed client code from a plugin's schema document (artifact) and **pinning** which schema version your client was built with. Thanks to pinning, when the server updates the plugin your client notices the mismatch instead of silently talking with the wrong fields.

## What the schema document contains

`GET /v1/plugins/{plugin_id}/schema` and `artifacts/plugins/<id>.plugin.schema.json` in the repository are the same document. The top-level fields:

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `pluginId` | The plugin ID (for example `package-ride`) |
| `version` | The plugin version (for example `1.44.0`) |
| `schemaSha256` | The digest of the `schemas` section; **the schema pinning key** (below) |
| `protocolVersion` | The plugin protocol major version (`1` for now) |
| `codecProfile` | The wire encoding: `plugin-v1-decimal-int64` ([numbers](../reference/numbers-and-encoding.md)) |
| `schemas` | Input and output JSON schemas (component name → schema) |
| `queries`, `commands` | Operation definitions |
| `modules` | Realtime modules |

An operation definition carries these fields:

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `name` | The operation name; it is the method name in client code (for example `claimLoginBonus`) |
| `kind`, `method`, `path` | `command` or `query`; the HTTP method; the full path |
| `requestSchema`, `responseSchema`, `schemaVersion` | The input and output schemas and the `x-crx-schema-version` to send |
| `requestMessageType`, `responseMessageType` | The `{message_type}` in the path and the output message type |
| `access` | `player`, `server`, or `public` |
| `retry` | `read` (safe to repeat), `same-key-command` (repeat only with the same key), `never` |
| `journal` | `none`, `full`, or `reference`: how the SDK records the command locally (to continue after a reload) |
| `idempotencyRequired`, `receiptRetentionSeconds`, `resultPath` | For commands: whether the key is required, the record retention, the result query path |
| `deprecated` | Deprecation information if present: `since`, `sinceUnixS`, `replacement` ([versioning](../reference/versioning.md)) |

A realtime module definition carries `moduleId`, `input`, `state`, `events`, `signals`, `authorityMode`, `deliveryClass`, `interestPolicy`, `stateTtlMs`, and `sequencePolicy`; the input and state messages have `maxBytes`, `ratePerSecond`, and `burst` ([protocol](../realtime/protocol.md)).

## What is `schemaSha256`

`schemaSha256` is the SHA-256 digest of the **canonical JSON** (keys sorted, no whitespace, UTF-8) output of the `schemas` object in the document (lowercase hexadecimal, 64 characters). If the schema fields change, the digest changes; even if the version number stays the same. Therefore saying "the version matches" is not enough: the **digest** must match.

You can also recompute and verify the digest yourself:

<!-- snippet: examples/ts/plugin-schema-hash.ts -->
```ts
import { createHash } from "node:crypto";

type Json = null | boolean | number | string | Json[] | { [key: string]: Json };

/** Anahtarları özyinelemeli sıralar; sunucudaki kurallı JSON biçimiyle aynı baytları üretir (boşluksuz). */
function canonical(value: Json): Json {
  if (Array.isArray(value)) return value.map(canonical);
  if (value !== null && typeof value === "object") {
    const sorted: { [key: string]: Json } = {};
    for (const key of Object.keys(value).sort()) sorted[key] = canonical(value[key] as Json);
    return sorted;
  }
  return value;
}

/** Şema belgesinin `schemas` bölümünden `schemaSha256` değerini yeniden hesaplar. */
export function schemaSha256(artifact: { schemas: Record<string, Json> }): string {
  return createHash("sha256").update(JSON.stringify(canonical(artifact.schemas)), "utf8").digest("hex");
}

/** Belgenin kendi bildirdiği özet, içeriğiyle uyuşuyor mu? */
export function artifactIsConsistent(artifact: { schemaSha256: string; schemas: Record<string, Json> }): boolean {
  return schemaSha256(artifact) === artifact.schemaSha256;
}
```

> [!NOTE]
> This computation covers only the `schemas` section; it does not cover the operation list (`commands`, `queries`) or the `version` field. If an operation's path or access type changes, the digest may not change; so when regenerating the binding, also look at the difference in the whole document.

## Generating a TypeScript binding

1. Get the schema document and save it to a file. From the server: `GET /v1/plugins/{plugin_id}/schema` with a player session or a server key.
2. Read the digest from the document (`schemaSha256`) and **write it into your release manifest/CI**: this is your pin value.
3. Generate the binding with the SDK's `crx` command:

```bash
npx crx plugins generate \
  --input demo-game.plugin.schema.json \
  --schema-sha256 ffc38829f30ea0dd1b829f351f905854a9875806fbc8a2bbd591815c7ee52c2f \
  --output src/generated/demo-game.ts
```

4. In CI, check that the generated file is up to date; `--check` does **not write** the file, it compares bytes:

```bash
npx crx plugins generate --input demo-game.plugin.schema.json \
  --schema-sha256 ffc38829f30ea0dd1b829f351f905854a9875806fbc8a2bbd591815c7ee52c2f \
  --output src/generated/demo-game.ts --check
```

Behavior:

- The input document is a local file; the command does not reach the network and does not execute the document.
- `--schema-sha256` is **required** and must be the same as the `schemaSha256` in the document; if it differs, generation is rejected. The digest is not refreshed automatically: you approve the change deliberately.
- The output writes only a new file; it does not overwrite an existing file (to regenerate, delete the file). The output folder must exist beforehand and its path must not contain a symbolic link (the command compares the path with its real path); symbolic links and special files are rejected; the target must be `.ts`. The input file can be at most 2 MiB.
- On success it prints `CRX PLUGINS GENERATE PASS` (with `--check`, `CRX PLUGINS CHECK PASS`) and exits with `0`.
- On failure the command gives no details (so that paths and contents do not leak); it prints a single line `CRX TYPES FAIL: input/version/hash/output reddedildi` and exits with `1`. First check the input file, the digest, and the output path.
- The same input always produces the same byte output.

The generated file carries the TypeScript types from the schemas (`int64` as `bigint`), the operation tables, the module definitions, and the embedded artifact + digest. Usage:

```ts
import { DemoGameBinding } from "./generated/demo-game.js";

const demo = client.plugin(DemoGameBinding);
await demo.queries.getGiftStatus({ includeHistory: true });
const handle = await demo.commands.claimDailyGift.prepare({ giftKey: "daily" });
```

Operations with `access: "server"` are **not visible in the player client** and cannot be called; call them over HTTP from your server with a server key ([calling plugins](calling-plugins.md)). `access: "public"` queries are called without identity with `game.publicPlugin(...)`.

> [!NOTE]
> If a command definition is `retry: "same-key-command"`, the SDK retries on a network error only **with the same key**; if it is `journal: "full"`, an unfinished command is continued with the same key when the page is reloaded. SDK installation and journal options are on the [TypeScript SDK](../sdk/typescript.md) page.

## Pinning and mismatch

The client checks version compatibility in two places:

1. **Build/startup time, local:** when setting up a binding with `createGameClient`, you provide the expectation (`pin`) from your game's release manifest. If the `pluginId`, `schemaSha256`, `protocolVersion`, `codecProfile` (and `version` if you provided it) in the binding do not match the expectation **exactly**, it throws `CrxError` (`kind: upgrade_required`, `code: plugin_binding_mismatch`); no request goes out. This check does not ask the server; it only verifies that the binding you hold is the binding you expect.
2. **The real document on the server:** the TypeScript SDK does **not** call the `/schema` endpoint on its own at runtime. To see a mismatch with your client's old binding in case the server updated the plugin, query the document yourself at startup and compare the digest:

<!-- snippet: examples/ts/plugin-pin-check.ts -->
```ts
import { DemoGameBinding } from "./generated/demo-game.js";

/**
 * Sunucudaki şema belgesini alır ve elinizdeki bağlamayla karşılaştırır. Uyuşmazsa istemciyi güncelleyin:
 * yeni belgeyi alın, yeni `schemaSha256`'yı manifestinize yazın, bağlamayı yeniden üretin.
 * Çerez oturumu açıkken tarayıcıda çalışır (aynı kökenden, `credentials: "include"`).
 */
export async function serverSchemaMatches(baseUrl: string, environmentId: string): Promise<boolean> {
  const response = await fetch(`${baseUrl}/v1/plugins/${DemoGameBinding.pluginId}/schema`, {
    credentials: "include",
    headers: { "x-crx-env": environmentId },
  });
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`şema belgesi alınamadı: HTTP ${response.status}`);
  const live = (await response.json()) as {
    pluginId: string;
    schemaSha256: string;
    protocolVersion: number;
    codecProfile: string;
  };
  return (
    live.pluginId === DemoGameBinding.pluginId &&
    live.schemaSha256 === DemoGameBinding.schemaSha256 &&
    live.protocolVersion === DemoGameBinding.protocolVersion &&
    live.codecProfile === DemoGameBinding.codecProfile
  );
}
```

In the Unity SDK this check is ready-made: `PluginClient.VerifyServerSchemaAsync()` gets the server's document and compares the `pluginId`, `schemaSha256`, `protocolVersion`, `codecProfile` (and `version` if given in the pin) fields with the binding's pin. On a mismatch it throws `OpenPlayException` (`ErrorKind.UpgradeRequired`, code `plugin_binding_mismatch`); if the pin field is missing or broken, `ErrorKind.Contract`.

<!-- snippet: examples/unity/PluginPinCheck.cs -->
```csharp
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using OpenPlay;
using OpenPlay.PackageRide;

/// <summary>
/// Oyuna girerken sunucudaki eklenti şemasının, pakete gömülü bağlamayla aynı olduğunu doğrular.
/// Uyuşmazlıkta (<c>UpgradeRequired</c>, kod <c>plugin_binding_mismatch</c>) oyuncuyu güncellemeye yönlendirin.
/// </summary>
public static class PluginPinCheck
{
    public static async Task<bool> ServerSchemaMatches(OpenPlayClient client)
    {
        var rides = PackageRideClient.Create(client); // pin: pluginId, schemaSha256, protocolVersion, codecProfile, version
        try
        {
            await rides.Plugin.VerifyServerSchemaAsync(); // GET /v1/plugins/package-ride/schema; yalnız pin alanlarını okur
            return true;
        }
        catch (OpenPlayException ex) when (ex.Kind == ErrorKind.UpgradeRequired)
        {
            UnityEngine.Debug.LogWarning("Eklenti şeması değişti; istemciyi güncelleyin: " + ex.Code);
            return false;
        }
    }
}
```

### What the client sees on a mismatch

If the server updates the plugin and your client calls with the old binding:

| Change | The client sees |
|---|---|
| The operation's `schemaVersion` value increased | `400` `invalid_argument` (unknown schema version). `x-crx-schema-version` was sending the old one |
| The operation or message type was removed | `404` `not_found` |
| A required field was added to the input schema | `400` `invalid_argument` (a payload that does not conform to the schema) |
| The output schema changed | The SDK cannot decode the response according to the binding; an error of type `contract` |
| The plugin is not enabled for your environment / the version grant does not match | `403` `forbidden` |

The fix is always the same: get the current schema document, deliberately write the new `schemaSha256` into your manifest, regenerate the binding, and ship the client. If old clients need to keep working for a while longer, ask your plugin provider whether the old schema version is kept on the server; the platform has no guarantee of this.

## Unity binding

In the Unity SDK, the binding comes ready **only for the `package-ride` plugin** (`OpenPlay.PackageRide`: `PackageRideBinding`, `PackageRideClient`). A Unity binding for another plugin cannot be generated today: the SDK's generator script is an in-repository tool and does not ship in the published package. The generator packages only operations whose `access` value is `player` or `public` (server-only operations do not enter the package) and accepts only the `plugin-v1-decimal-int64` encoding; `int64` fields become C# `long`.

For usage, see the `PluginCall` example on the [Unity SDK](../sdk/unity.md) page.

